Allow custom width and height.

Allow DPI selection:

- 72
- 96
- 150
- 300
- Custom

For print-oriented projects, support appropriate physical dimensions and resolution.

Do not confuse pixel dimensions with physical print dimensions.

## 4.2 Canvas behavior

Implement:

- Zoom in
- Zoom out
- Fit canvas
- Actual pixel size
- Rotate canvas
- Reset rotation
- Flip canvas horizontally
- Flip canvas vertically
- Pan canvas
- Scroll navigation
- Canvas centering
- Grid display
- Rulers
- Guides
- Symmetry guides

The canvas must remain responsive during drawing.

Use appropriate coordinate transformations.

The mouse position must map accurately to document coordinates regardless of zoom or canvas rotation.

## 4.3 Canvas navigation

Support:

- Mouse wheel zoom
- Space + drag for pan
- Middle mouse button pan
- Keyboard zoom shortcuts
- Navigator panel
- Zoom percentage display

Canvas navigation must not accidentally create brush strokes.

## 4.4 Large canvas support

The software must be designed to handle large documents.

Do not assume that every document will fit into RAM as a single uncompressed image.

Consider:

- Tiled rendering
- Dirty-region updates
- Cached previews
- Lazy loading
- Background processing
- Memory monitoring

Use a tile-based rendering architecture when necessary.

Avoid redrawing the entire canvas for every brush movement.

## 4.5 Canvas performance

Implement:

- Partial repainting
- Render caching
- Efficient image buffers
- Background processing for expensive operations
- UI-thread protection
- Resource cleanup

The interface must remain responsive during long operations.

